\section{A Block Names a Continuation}

An SSA block can be read as a local function describing what happens next. Andrew Appel's \emph{SSA is Functional Programming} uses this correspondence to relate dominance in a flow graph to lexical scope. Give each block a function name and turn its $\phi$ results into formal parameters. A branch edge supplies the corresponding incoming argument tuple. Within a block an ordinary instruction becomes a binding, and the terminator invokes another local function in tail position or returns from the enclosing procedure. A conditional chooses which function to invoke; it does not evaluate both successors.

By default, put all local function labels in a recursive binding group and pass live-in values explicitly if they would otherwise fall out of scope. A more economical presentation nests functions according to dominance, allowing an inner block to refer to an already bound value from a dominating block. Such a reference does not imply allocation of a runtime closure. A known, nonescaping label reached by jumps has no first-class identity to maintain. Conversely, an incoming $\phi$ operand need not dominate the join. It only has to be in scope at the particular call site that supplies it.

\section{Translate a Loop Both Ways}

Here is a loop summing the integers from zero up to, but not including, \texttt{n}, with \texttt{n} a parameter of the procedure. Assume exact arithmetic for this example.

\begin{verbatim}
entry:  goto head
head:   i = phi(entry: 0, body: next_i)
        s = phi(entry: 0, body: next_s)
        if i < n then goto body else goto exit
body:   next_i = i + 1
        next_s = s + i
        goto head
exit:   return s
\end{verbatim}

The two loop parameters can be written as a single recursive local function, with the two single-predecessor blocks written inline:

\begin{verbatim}
letrec head(i, s) =
  if i < n then
    let next_i = i + 1 in
    let next_s = s + i in
    tail head(next_i, next_s)
  else s
in tail head(0, 0)
\end{verbatim}

Reading backwards, make a block for \texttt{head}, a $\phi$ for each formal, and one predecessor argument list per call edge. The call has simultaneous binding semantics: on the backedge, \texttt{next\_s} uses the old \texttt{i}, not the already incremented value. With \texttt{n = 3} the header states are $(0,0),(1,0),(2,1),(3,3)$. A variant that updates the header's storage for \texttt{i} before computing the sum reaches the wrong result. This test makes the functional account of edges useful even if the compiler never emits a function call for them (Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-207}).

\section{Where the Correspondence Stops}

Richard Kelsey's \emph{A Correspondence between Continuation Passing Style and Static Single Assignment Form} gives syntactic translations for appropriate CPS and SSA languages. The direct block reading applies to local jump continuations with known targets, invoked in tail position. It does not turn every general CPS value into an intraprocedural label. An escaping continuation can be stored, passed to another procedure, or invoked after the source activation would otherwise have ended. A plain CFG edge cannot describe that lifetime or account for all the potential transfers. Even ordinary procedure calls need a calling convention and a representation of their normal and exceptional return points.

The word ``functional'' describes the binding discipline here, not the absence of effects. In a state-passing account, a store consumes the current memory state and yields its successor; an effectful call can likewise return a value and a state. Tail-calling the right continuation means passing both results associated with the actual edge. Without such explicit state, the translation must retain the sequencing of the original operations. Lexical availability of two loads is not grounds to reorder them around a store. The dossier's Chapter~16, \emph{Effect-Aware IR}, makes this separation of value and effect contracts explicit.

\section{Choose a Notation That Exposes Rewrite Obligations}

When a join is written as a parameterized label, some graph rewrites become easier to state. A parameter with no uses can be removed together with its argument at every call site. An argument passed unchanged by all incoming edges can instead be captured when its definition dominates the label; otherwise the apparent agreement is not enough to introduce the free reference. Block argument lists also make edge redirection an interface change: the replacement edge must supply the right types and arity, including any effect state. A round-trip test should compare branch behavior and observable effects under renaming, not demand that the starting graph be recovered verbatim: inlining \texttt{body} and \texttt{exit} in the example already changed that graph.
